# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Maximum of two numbers".
id: maximum
title: Maximum of two numbers
topic: Conditional
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Maximum of two numbers"
tags: conditional, wp, consequence

task:
  Compute by hand the weakest precondition of the following program,
  which stores in $m$ the larger of $x$ and $y$. Deduce that the program
  is correct with respect to its contract.

pre: true
post: m >= x && m >= y && (m = x || m = y)

program:
  {P}
  if x >= y then {
    {P && x >= y}
    {R1}
    m := x           // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {P && !(x >= y)}
    {R2}
    m := y           // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The rule for the conditional
rule: IF
text:
  The rule IF has two premises, one per branch. In the then branch we may
  assume the condition $x >= y$, and in the else branch its negation.
instance: IF
  {true && x >= y} m := x {Q}
  {true && !(x >= y)} m := y {Q}
  ---
  {true} if x >= y then m := x else m := y {Q}
text:
  We prove each premise with the assignment rule and the consequence rule.
  The annotated program shows the two branch preconditions, followed by
  the weakest preconditions $R1$ and $R2$ that we compute now.

step: The then branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
calc:
  wp(m := x, Q)
  = x >= x && x >= y && (x = x || x = y)
  = true && x >= y && true      // x >= x and x = x are always true
  = x >= y
set: R1 = x >= y
vc: true && x >= y ==> x >= y
why:
  In the then branch the condition $x >= y$ holds, which is exactly $R1$.

step: The else branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
calc:
  wp(m := y, Q)
  = y >= x && y >= y && (y = x || y = y)
  = y >= x
set: R2 = y >= x
vc: true && !(x >= y) ==> y >= x
why:
  $!(x >= y)$ means $x < y$, and $x < y$ implies $y >= x$.

step: The same result with the wp of the conditional
rule: WP, LOGIC
text:
  The wp calculus combines the two branches into one formula. It gives the
  same verification conditions, written as one implication.
calc:
  wp(if x >= y then m := x else m := y, Q)
  = (x >= y ==> x >= y) && (!(x >= y) ==> y >= x)
  = true && true
  = true
vc: true ==> (x >= y ==> x >= y) && (!(x >= y) ==> y >= x)
why:
  Both implications are true in every state, so the weakest precondition
  is $true$ and any precondition implies it.
result: The triple is valid for every initial state.
